SecureCRT setup

Create a new SSH2 session in SecureCRT.

Use this configuration:

SecureCRT field	Value
Protocol	SSH2
Hostname	PSMP_Server_FQDN
Port	22
Username	<VaultUser>@<GuardiumCLIUser>@<GuardiumApplianceFQDN>
Authentication	Password, Keyboard Interactive, or SSH key depending on your CyberArk setup

Example:

Hostname: psmp01.company.com
Port: 22
Username: ben.akin@company.com@guardcli1@guardium01.company.com

For your IBM Guardium CLI use case:

<VaultUser>@guardcli1@<Guardium_Appliance>@<PSMP_Server>

In SecureCRT, because the hostname is entered separately, the username field should normally be:

<VaultUser>@guardcli1@<Guardium_Appliance>

Then SecureCRT connects to:

PSMP_Server_FQDN

CyberArk also notes that PSM for SSH can authenticate the user to the Vault using password, SSH key, or other supported methods, and then transparently retrieve the vaulted target credential for privileged SSO.

PuTTY setup

In PuTTY:

PuTTY field	Value
Host Name	PSMP_Server_FQDN
Port	22
Connection type	SSH
Auto-login username	<VaultUser>@guardcli1@<GuardiumApplianceFQDN>

Example:

Host Name: psmp01.company.com
Port: 22
Auto-login username: ben.akin@company.com@guardcli1@guardium01.company.com

Equivalent command line:

putty.exe -ssh ben.akin@company.com@guardcli1@guardium01.company.com@psmp01.company.com

For OpenSSH:

ssh ben.akin@company.com@guardcli1@guardium01.company.com@psmp01.company.com
Recommended Guardium-specific pattern

For IBM Guardium, I would onboard the appliance accounts like this in CyberArk:

CyberArk account field	Example
Username	guardcli1
Address	guardium01.company.com
Platform	IBM_Guardium_CLI_SSH
Connection Component	PSMP-SSH
Safe	Guardium CLI Safe

Then give users this SecureCRT format:

Hostname: <PSMP_Server>
Username: <CyberArkUser>@guardcli1@<GuardiumAppliance>
